In article <3AE70F5A.B26F320@gsfc.nasa.gov>, Robert DiRosario <robert.j.dirosario.1@gsfc.nasa.gov> writes:
:What is the difference between the QDSS and QVSS graphic boards?

  QVSS (aka VCB01) is a single-board monochrome Q-bus graphics controller.
  The QVSS is not supported on most recent OpenVMS versions.

  QDSS (aka VCB02, aka GPX; uses the Dragon graphics chipset) is a two- or 
  three-board greyscale or color graphics controller -- the base module 
  plus up to two four plane boards can be configured, for a total of up 
  to eight planes...

  The VCB03 was an M-bus-based version of the GPX board that was used on 
  the Firefox (VAXstation 3520, VAXstation 3540 series) systems, and is 
  NOT compatible with the Q-bus workstation systems.

:If I take a QDSS board set from a BA23 system and put it in a VAX 3800,
:will I get a VAX Station 3800?  I know I'll need to cut a hole in a
:cover plate to install the connector, but other than that will it work?

  The older revision QDSS boards require an ECO for use on KA650 and 
  KA655 processors -- without the ECO, you will see the Q-bus lock up.  
  Off-hand, I think you need at least revision D1 of the base module -- 
  the higher the revision, the better.   (For hardware support and such, 
  see the Compaq Assisted Services program information...)

  There was an I/O bulkhead available for the QDSS module in the S-box 
  BA213 series enclosure -- the QDSS was sold and supported in the BA213 
  series enclosure, and the most common example of a QDSS in a system in 
  the BA213 series was known as the VAXstation 3500.


