In article <1010426014457.5995A@Ives.egh.com>, John Santos <JOHN@egh.com> writes:

:Reading between the lines... What we are discussing here is a way for
:an (Alpha?) VMS system to function as a LAD server, at least for CD-ROM.

  No need to infer.  Yes, we are providing a LAD server capability for 
  OpenVMS for specified chunks of disk storage on the OpenVMS server.
  We should be able to serve CD-ROM or other stuff, with it likely that
  we will be able to serve the contents of a partition (container file)
  on an OpenVMS host disk.)

:Meaning that any (VAX or Alpha) VMS system which is able to boot from a
:LAD-served disk (i.e. a LAD client) could boot from a LAD-served CD
:(i.e. a VMS Vx.y (x.y>7.3) O/S CD) mounted on the LAD server, just by
:being capable of performing a MOP (ethernet) boot. 
..
:Meaning you don't need a local CD-ROM drive on your old, non-SCSI VAX 
:to install or upgrade VMS or layered products (or to boot S/A backup?).

  Most of these old VAX systems are NOT capable of network bootstraps.
  You WILL need to have a bootable disk or bootable tape available.

:I seem to recall a thread a few months ago where someone, maybe you,
:said that after V7.3, CD-ROM would be the only distibution medium.
:No more TK50/9-track/RX01/RA60/???.

  Correct.  Current OpenVMS VAX kits are available on TK50 media,
  nine-track 6250 BPI, and CD-ROM.  After V7.3, OpenVMS VAX kits
  will be available only on CD-ROM.  

  Why?  DLT-I media (TK52 cartridges) is simply becoming unavailable.  

  And no -- as one wag recently offered -- I don't want to receive 
  crates of slightly-used TK50 media.

:Is this to make all us VAX dinosaurs happy?

  Donno.  Various reasons for this can include but are not limited to, 
  and in no particular order, helping with the end of the availability 
  of the TK50 media, due to the requirements of the factory installed 
  software process within Compaq, with customer requests, and to help
  the folks with aging InfoServer devices.

:Of course, once everyone is switched to CD, all the new systems
:will start shipping with DVD instead.  :-)

  We expect to be shipping OpenVMS Alpha on DVD sometime after V7.3.

:Will this be a separately licensed product or included in the
:base VMS license?  If licensed, will the PAK be included in the
:hobbyist licenses?

  AFAIK, current plans involve including this into OpenVMS.

:Which VAXes can be booted from the ethernet?  All of them or
:only the ones whose boot code is more recent than the existence
:of Ethernet adapters?  (Does this depend only on VMB, which would
:mean that even an 11/780 with a current console floppy could
:boot this way?)

  The list of CD-ROM "deprived" VAX systems could include the following:

    VAX-11/725, VAX-11/730, VAX-11/750, VAX-11/780, VAX-11/785, VAXstation
    8000, VAX 8200, VAX 8250, VAX 8300, VAX 8350, VAX 8500, VAX 8530,
    VAX 8550, VAX 8600, VAX 8650, VAX 8700, VAX 8800, VAX 8800, VAX 8810,
    VAX 8820, VAX 8820N, VAX 8830, VAX 8840.  

  Probably a few others I've missed.

  Most large-scale VAX systems -- well, VAX systems that were considered
  large-scale when they were released -- lack MOP download capabilities 
  in the console.  This includes most VAX systems that were released prior 
  to the InfoServer (eg: systems older than the VAX 6000 series).

  The VAX 6000, VAX 9000 series, and other later large-scale VAX systems 
  WITH console support for MOP downloads use the ISL_LVAX.SYS module.
  IIRC, all MicroVAX and VAX 4000 series systems provide console support 
  for MOP downloads, and all use the ISL_SVAX.SYS module for the download.
  (The ISL_LVAX module is effectively VMB with some extra "stuff" needed 
  for the download service selection process.  The ISL_SVAX module is VMB 
  with NISCS.  Additionally, the ISL_LVAX.SYS module is not loaded by and
  does not depend on a console-based version of VMB, while ISL_SVAX.SYS is 
  loaded by the console VMB.)

:To look a gift horse in the mouth, what about a LAD server that 
:could run on a PC?

  I am aware of no plans to provide LAD/LAST protocol specifications,
  nor any plans to provide a PC-based InfoServer.

