"pat jankowiak" <vaxhackery@worldnet.att.net> wrote in message
news:3AE50655.3F33469B@worldnet.att.net...
> The fans in a 6000 are DC and run off the power supply. The 6530
> I have is 208 wye. I think this means 110VAC between any two
> phases and 208 to neutral. One thing of note is that the neutral
> does not go into the rectifier. it is I think used in the power
> control box, where some relays live.
>
Actually, it's 208 between legs of the wye and 120 from any leg to "neutral"
(center point of the wye).

Remember that a wye xformer is fed a single sine wave: the crest rotates
around the legs of the wye, giving you the A-B-C phase legs, 120 degrees
apart, and the neutral hub. One critical piece of protective equipment for
3-phase computer systems not protected by a UPS is a phase-rotation fault
detector. If you lose one leg of the wye, it's supposed to pop the main
protective disconnector to protect the load.

Dave Schmitt

P.S.: As long as we're talking plant stuff...how many folks got extended-run
UPS and/or gensets? How many put their critical load HVAC systems on the
genset as well?





