...

> I certainly think that a cut in licensing costs would give sales a real
> shot in the arm. However, I doubt if the current economic climate would
> make it seem like a wise move to the top people.

That really ought to depend on whether they're currently making any
non-negligible profit on the lowest-end systems:  if not, then there's
nothing significant to lose, as long as they don't take a noticeable loss on
each system.

People have occasionally conjectured that having too low an entry-level
price could cause cannibalization of higher-end systems by clusters of
low-end systems, but that could be limited by fiat (enforced in the code) if
necessary - and they've already started offering clustering sub-set
functionality at lower prices (though how useful that is may be in
question).

The real problem at this point may be hardware pricing:  as long as the
hardware platform costs at least twice as much as a high-quality Intel (or
better, AMD) platform of somewhat similar capabilities, it's not clear that
the VMS license pricing is all that much out of line, though reducing it
wouldn't hurt (again, assuming there's currently not enough volume in that
segment to generate much profit anyway).

- bill





