> by clusters of low-end systems, but that could be limited by fiat
> (enforced in the code) if necessary - and they've already started
> offering clustering sub-set functionality at lower prices (though
> how useful that is may be in question).

Any one is going to use as low a cost systems as is reasonable. What
is harder to pick is when someone nedds, or has a good call for the
'big box' function, but not the grunt. So he is screwed, and often
knows that what he needs is there, just can't use it with out a wad of
$$. How to piss off customers, step 1.

> The real problem at this point may be hardware pricing: as long as
> the hardware platform costs at least twice as much as a high-quality
> Intel (or better, AMD) platform of somewhat similar capabilities,
> it's not clear that the VMS license pricing is all that much out of
> line, though reducing it wouldn't hurt (again, assuming there's
> currently not enough volume in that segment to generate much profit
> anyway).

With hard ware, you have real money at stake. You can't stamp out
DS10Es for 20c each! Hell, even if the box was Tooth Faired to the
door step for nix, there is still a lot of money in stocking, shipping
and installing.  Plus spares inventory. Real money, mind, money that
must be paid to someone.

Licences are essentially free. Some arbitary amount of admin overhead,
and a bit of paper. So it should be of the order of $25-50 plus and
extra $1-2 per extra licence in one hit. That is COST, real money. So
if you give some one a $20,000 licence, you are $50 out of pocket. If
you give him a $3000 box, that will be, on VMS current margins, $1500
out of pocket.

(Note that I'm just handwaving the media kits, OK Given a descent
pressing run, the cost also falls into the noise.)

So how do you spice things up at the moment? Cut to the bone where it
will not COST you, even if that throws away potential revenue.  If you
do it well, that money WILL come back in a few months, plus you
perhaps could claim an increase in 'System' sales, so you are on of
the few not slidding into the mud. Being the only vendor with growth
in a recession is priceless for punter confidence.

Also, note well that I'm refering to licence costs above, not support
costs. It may well be that you need to add a support fee to the day
one cost. Of course, if you want 24/7, 1hr max fix, that will cost a
'little' more :) And that is where the money really is!!

