"Paul Repacholi" <prep@prep.synonet.com> wrote in message
news:87pue4n7h0.fsf@prep.synonet.com...

...

> Any one is going to use as low a cost systems as is reasonable. What
> is harder to pick is when someone nedds, or has a good call for the
> 'big box' function, but not the grunt. So he is screwed, and often
> knows that what he needs is there, just can't use it with out a wad of
> $$. How to piss off customers, step 1.

This is called supply and demand (or, alternatively, value), something we in
the U.S. often consider to be a Good Thing.  It motivates both innovation
and self-improvement by 'have-nots' to become 'haves' so that they can
purchase such goodies, and at the same time is mitigated by the fact that
anything that is *in actuality* over-priced for its value will be the target
of competitive products at more reasonable prices.

>
> > The real problem at this point may be hardware pricing: as long as
> > the hardware platform costs at least twice as much as a high-quality
> > Intel (or better, AMD) platform of somewhat similar capabilities,
> > it's not clear that the VMS license pricing is all that much out of
> > line, though reducing it wouldn't hurt (again, assuming there's
> > currently not enough volume in that segment to generate much profit
> > anyway).
>
> With hard ware, you have real money at stake. You can't stamp out
> DS10Es for 20c each! Hell, even if the box was Tooth Faired to the
> door step for nix, there is still a lot of money in stocking, shipping
> and installing.  Plus spares inventory. Real money, mind, money that
> must be paid to someone.
>
> Licences are essentially free. Some arbitary amount of admin overhead,
> and a bit of paper. So it should be of the order of $25-50 plus and
> extra $1-2 per extra licence in one hit. That is COST, real money. So
> if you give some one a $20,000 licence, you are $50 out of pocket. If
> you give him a $3000 box, that will be, on VMS current margins, $1500
> out of pocket.

But if you sell, say, 10,000 licenses at cost ($50), but would still have
sold, say, 2000 of them at $20,000 apiece, you're out (really out:  this is
money you would have had) $39.5 million.

That's what value is all about, and it's only tenuously related to actual
manufacturing/handling cost (though it does have something to do with
overall cost, including development, because that's what allows competition
to spring up if you price things too much out of proportion).  And that's
why the platform cost *is* relevant, because as long as the license cost is
not too unreasonable as a percentage of the total system cost it won't
affect sales volume all that much.

>
> (Note that I'm just handwaving the media kits, OK Given a descent
> pressing run, the cost also falls into the noise.)
>
> So how do you spice things up at the moment? Cut to the bone where it
> will not COST you, even if that throws away potential revenue.  If you
> do it well, that money WILL come back in a few months,

That's easy for you to say:  it's not your business on the line if you're
wrong.

If Compaq in fact *is* generating significant revenue with its current
entry-level systems and pricing, then the portion of that revenue derived
from license prices is at risk.  And whether it would make up anywhere
nearly that amount in increased hardware revenue (actually, profit from
those increased sales, and perhaps from later service) is a function of the
detailed shape of the demand curve, which it's extremely unlikely you have
any real insight into at that level of detail (even very thorough market
analysis can get it wrong, and often does).

Conversely, if no significant revenue is being generated in this segment
today, then there's no significant risk to dropping license prices and
seeing what happens.  Which is what I said.

- bill





