Malcolm MacArthur wrote:

> Paul Sture wrote:
> [...]
> >
> > The UK does have a ground to earth. Sometimes just a rod into the ground,
> > but I've seen it tied to water pipes, and gas pipes as well. Whether the
> > latter is just a good ground or a way of ensuring that gas and electric
> > charge don't mix I honestly don't know.
>
> Sometimes, ground can be tied to neutral instead. Which leads me to an
> amusing
> story about a former flatmate. He had just moved in and plugged in his
> TV - only
> to find out it didn't work at all.
>
> After much headscratching, he asked me, and after further headscratching
> I sai
> we should open up the plug and try replacing the fuse, as it might have
> blown or
> broken in transit. So I duly opened up the plug to find that the neutral
> wire
> was connected to earth! Rewired the plug and TV worked OK.
>
> It could only have worked if his house had ground tied to neutral -
> correct?

No, it means his house had a bad ground connection. As pointed out earlier,
neutral is connected to ground, but quite often only at the source = the power
companies big transformer. So it should be possible to run equipment with a
connection between hot and ground. Maybe you know those small screwdrivers with
a small neon light in them. You can use them to check if a wire is 'hot'. They
(should) only light up when you touch the hot wire, never when you touch
neutral, because there is suppose to be no (big) voltage difference between
neutral and ground.



