> "Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> writes:
>
> > This is called supply and demand (or, alternatively, value), something
we in
> > the U.S. often consider to be a Good Thing.  It motivates both
innovation
> > and self-improvement by 'have-nots' to become 'haves' so that they can
> > purchase such goodies, and at the same time is mitigated by the fact
that
> > anything that is *in actuality* over-priced for its value will be the
target
> > of competitive products at more reasonable prices.
>
> This can only be true if the market is in what I, as a physicist, would
call
> thermal equilibrium. This is not the case for microprocessors and
systems -
> in economic speak, the price of entry into the market is so high that the
> existing players can play oligopoly for all they want - it doesn't matter
> whether they're doing it conciously or not. x86 systems are a somewhat
> different matter, because of AMD, and indirectly influence this market of
> course.

It's been a very long time since I looked at a thermodynamics text, but IIRC
(and I might not) being in thermal equilibrium does not require constant
temperature across the entire domain (certainly not at the atomic level,
though that may not enter into the formal evaluation save statistically),
just reliable movement toward that state, possibly after occasional external
perturbations.  The rate at which the state is approached is a function of
the medium's conductivity - and in the case of an oligopoly in a
high-cost-of-entry industry, the effective conductivity of the medium is
simply relatively low.

I did mention such considerations later in my post.  And I tend to think
that as long as governments place some limits on real monopolistic abuse,
over reasonable time periods the system works fairly well in balancing
society's interest in innovation against its interest in making
reasonably-priced products available to its citizenry (not to mention any
issues of individual freedom involved, which can extend to the freedom to
form groups - corporations - to engage in profitable activity).  When
discussions enter life-and-death products in the medical arena I must
confess to having some reservations, but I don't place computers in the same
category.

- bill

