In Article <3AE2EEF7.1B7161B8@iee.org>
"antonio.carlini" <arcarlini@iee.org> writes:
>Paul Sture wrote:
>> Got carried away counting the wires. 3 wires - Live, Neutral and Earth.
>> 240V AC. Neutral is a definite misnomer as it can give a shock.
>
>It shouldn't shock under normal conditions - it's usually tied
>to ground further upstream.
>
>> The UK does have a ground to earth. Sometimes just a rod into the ground,
>> but I've seen it tied to water pipes, and gas pipes as well. Whether the
>> latter is just a good ground or a way of ensuring that gas and electric
>> charge don't mix I honestly don't know.
>
>We certainly do have an earth and there are various wiring
>schemes described in the IEE regs. But I've never seen anything
>equivalent to the US centre ground where you get 220V in
>effectively split as two 110V supplies.

Well, the problem is that the center wire in US 110/220 V wiring is not
a ground at all.  It is the Neutral wire, the "Ground" wire is supposed
to be a totally separate wire.  The proper wiring for a 110 V circuit
in the US has three wires, "Hot", Neutral and Ground.  Depending on the
local codes, a proper 220 V circuit can be done with 3 or 4 wires.  For
3 wires the the connected device can not have any internal circuits
using 110 V.  Otherwise 4 wires get used - 2 Hot, Neutral and Ground.
In all cases, US practice is the ground wire is a "protective" ground
connected to the case or cabinet of the electrical appliance.  It is
supposed to only carry current in case of an electrical fault, never at
any other time.  The reason the center wire is Neutral comes from how
the 220 V circuit is created at the street.  The 3 wires are connected
to the two ends of a transformer winding and a center tap.

    _____Hot1______________   __________________________
                           ) (            ^
                           ( )            |
    _____Neutral___________) (         line voltage
                           ( )            |
                           ) (            |
    _____Hot2______________( )            |
                             (            |
                             )            |
                             (____________v_____________


So between the "Hot1" and "Hot2" there is 220 V single phase, and 110 V
single phase between either Hot and the Neutral.  Inside a building, 
half the 110 V circuits are supposed to be on each side.  In practice
this is done in the breaker box with every other breaker position going
between the Neutral and one of the "Hots"  A 220 V breaker takes two
positions.

>
>As for using water and gas pipes as a ground, that's a definite
>no-no these days. Using gas pipes always struck me as
>an "interesting" idea even when it was allowed. Water pipes
>would be fine except these days you never know whether some way
>upstream it changes into plastic.
>
>A rod in the ground is still (I think) acceptable in some
>wiring regimes, assuming you cannot find a better ground
>in some other way.

Depends on local wiring codes, which usually reflect local needs to get
a good ground in soils commonly seen in the area.  For instance, when I
last checked several years ago, a ground rod was supposed to be a 1/2"
copper rod 3' long driven into the ground vertically until the top was
buried.  That works pretty well in the damp, high clay content soils we
see around my town, but might not be good enough in dry, sandy soils.

Joe Heimann

heimann@ecs.umass.edu


