Paul Repacholi wrote:
[...]
> Paul, is the cavity on that package near the full size of the chip? It
> seems to have gone from 3 package bonding pad 'steps' to about 7 or 8!

I don't know. The aluminum 0.25um/0.18 um hybrid EV68 (now shipping
at 833 MHz) die measures 13.1 x 14.7 mm. The IBM manufactured copper
"true" 0.18 um EV68 measures 10 x 12 mm, significantly smaller.

> 
> Re thermal resitance. How long are Intel and AMD going to be able to
> stay with the current low cost packages, and all the temp and heatsink
> problems that that engenders?

I don't know about AMD, they are an information black hole. But
Intel designers spent a lot of effort at dynamic power management
at just about every level of the P4. Even the system interface
uses a neat scheme to make sure that never more than 8 signals
within each 16 bit subdivision of the data bus changes state
during any data beat. They have a polarity indicator signal
for each 16 bit sub-group of data bus. If more than 8 bits within
a subgroup changes state then they flip the polarity signal
and invert all 16 bits within that group for transmission. The
chipset uses the polarity signal to know whent to complement
received data. Not earth shattering but every little bit helps.


