JF Mezei wrote:
> 
> Paul DeMone wrote:
> > HP ported it from their 0.5 um process to Intel's 0.25 um process
> > (PA-8500) it originally simulated maxxing out at 275 MHz (it ran
> > up to 180 MHz in 0.5 um). It took a heck of a lot of work to bring
> > it up to the 400 MHz targeted release frequency. It eventually
> > topped out at 440 MHz. More work and a Leff process tweak lifted it
> > to 552 MHz (PA-8600).
> 
> That is my point. Instead of waiting for the ALPHA to be fully ported to the
> new process, couldn't they have generated a "early port" with a slight
> increase in mhz without any redesign, and then later release the fine-tuned
> chip that can take full advantage of the new process ?

The hybrid 0.25/0.18 um EV68 now shipping at 833 MHz (and supposedly
scalable up to 1.- to 1.1 GHz) is exactly such a device. It uses
basically the same metal layout as the 0.25 um EV67 but is made in a
0.18 um process so the transistor drawn gate lengths can be narrowed
to 0.18 um. This doesn't give you the full performance of a true 0.18
um device but is certainly faster to market. Although for Alpha over
the last 3 years the term faster certainly is relative :-(

