Paul Sture wrote:
> Got carried away counting the wires. 3 wires - Live, Neutral and Earth.
> 240V AC. Neutral is a definite misnomer as it can give a shock.

It shouldn't shock under normal conditions - it's usually tied
to ground further upstream.

> The UK does have a ground to earth. Sometimes just a rod into the ground,
> but I've seen it tied to water pipes, and gas pipes as well. Whether the
> latter is just a good ground or a way of ensuring that gas and electric
> charge don't mix I honestly don't know.

We certainly do have an earth and there are various wiring
schemes described in the IEE regs. But I've never seen anything
equivalent to the US centre ground where you get 220V in
effectively split as two 110V supplies.

As for using water and gas pipes as a ground, that's a definite
no-no these days. Using gas pipes always struck me as
an "interesting" idea even when it was allowed. Water pipes
would be fine except these days you never know whether some way
upstream it changes into plastic.

A rod in the ground is still (I think) acceptable in some
wiring regimes, assuming you cannot find a better ground
in some other way.

Antonio

