>If you mean the ES45 , the answer is no AFAIK. It looks very much like a ES40,
>and that runs on single phase 230V of here in Europe. Although it can have up
>to 4 (?) power supplies, and it is possible to connect the power supplies to
>different phases. So in case one phase goes down, you can keep the machine
>running on the other phase. Over here a 19" cabinet usualy has 2 power cables
>with 32A CEE form single phase plugs coming from its switchbox, and we connect
>these cables to different phases.

fwiw, wrt es4x rack or pedestal units: 

- up to three "bulk" power supplies; and anything bigger than a quad *needs*
two supplies, with the third providing n+1 current sharing front-end beasties
per crate

- depending on memory population & #processors, each (of up to three) cords
into an ES4x crate can draw enough power that (for most services) it actually
*requires* the use of a unique mains branch per cord. 

- and unless *three* substation sources converge at the crate, only uni's and
duals could ever hope to obtain 100% mains redundancy (artifact of the triples
and quad's requiring two supplies be *always* functional). With only two
station  service, you'd be rolling the dice on every blackout.

btw, to the person that asked: any >1g CuEV is a bitch to air-cool in a
daughterboard (ala es4x) configuration. You really wouldn't want to drop an
ES45 heat sink on your foot...

/daytripper ("long" in aluminum and copper shares ;-)

