 you are correct that there is no principal need for three phase power
on cooking equipment. But even with 220-240V (as standard in europe for
1-phase lines), four cooking zones plus the oven/grill will draw a lot
of current (you have to calculate worst case). So our equipment comes
with 3 (separate) 220V power inputs. You can connect them all to one
phase if the fuse is strong enough, or can connect them to three
different 220V phases, effectively distributing the load. As (for
years/decades) houses/flats get three phases from the power companies,
the kitchen usually has one 3-phase outlet. Just make sure you don't mix
up phase and zero. Otherwise you will end up with 380V at a place where
they don't belong :-)

 You are of course also correct that 50 vs. 60 Hz does not matter for
cooking :-) The thermal inertia of the equipment is much to big.

 Now, what again has this to do with DEC/Compaq equipment - besides that
Alphas are running hot :-)

