>
> > It is my understanding that the standard U.S. household feed is a single
> > phase nominal 220 to 240 Volts.  It is center tapped to ground at the
> > local distribution transformer, allowing two 110 to 120 volt legs to be
> > distributed through the house.
> >
> > The maximum potential between a single wire and ground is about 120 V.
> >
> > Beyond the transformer may vary by region, but since in the rural areas
> > where I can see the wires, only two lines are going to the distribution
> > transformers on most residential streets.  This leads me to believe that
> > only a single phase is shared by that neighborhood from some upstream
> > feed point.
> >
> > Getting 3 phases to a house can mean requiring three wires from that
> > upstream feed point.  Sharing the existing phase could present potential
> > balancing problems for the distribution point, as the expectation would
> > be that you have a significant load to justify the expense.
> >
> >
> > Just following this thread has shown how much confusion there is out
> > there on electrical wiring.
> >
> > And do not take my word on how things are wired.  Look it up from
> > an authorative source.
>
> Your 120/240 description is correct, and you wisely avoided Delta
> vs. Wye descriptions for three-phase.  But, I did look up a source
> authoritative for my neighborhood, the pole out front :-).  On my
> street there is a three phase (four wire) 13,800 volt main feed
> across the top of the poles (I thought 13,900 volts, but a repair
> crew corrected my assumption one day.  Every block or so there is
> a transformer (pole pig) taking one of those phases to reduce it to
> the 120/240 single phase (sometimes called "two phase" at 240
> volts) feed for the next group of houses.  When I was growing up
> the 120/240 volt feed was always three wires one above each other
> from pole to pole, while the three phase wires were separate from
> each other between crossbars on the successive poles.
>
> These days, in Massachusetts anyway, replacement 120/240 feeds
> tend to have three wires twisted together while replacement
> three phase 13KV feeds tend to be separated on the corners
> of rectangular insulators about 8 inches diagonally.  My
> assumption is that this change is to resist damage from
> ice storms, so your experience in Florida may be different :-)

This message shows an important difference between the US and Europe. In most of Western Europe we don't have
power distribution-on-poles, at least not to houses.. The power cables are usually dug in the ground, hence
the transformers can be bigger and fewer (and 3 phase). In the US power distribution cables hang freely along
the streets, at least in more rural areas. During a short visit to the US I went to Key West. It is amazing
to see how the beautiful look of a very nice town can be destroyed by these power and telephone cables
dangling along the streets.





