That is good news, if it is so.

> Intel seems to have very little difficulty in increasing the speed
> of its 8086 at a rapid pace. is it correct to state that they get
> about 2 speed increases per year ? (in Mhz measures, not necessarily
> in performance).

I don't think 2x per year is accurate. This many be the case in a
single year period, a late ship, and an early follow on could do this.

> Is there anything would would have prevented Compaq from producing
> 1ghz versions of the existing alpha chip, just like Intel produced
> 1+ ghz versions of its pentium III ?

> I realise that the upcoming chip incldes design improvements, but
> from a pure mhz point of view, must the design be changed to allow
> greater speed, or just the manufacturing process ? (with perhaps
> minor tewaking of design).

In principle, just re-layout and go! Practise is a fair bit narkier...
First off, although we generally see a single number for feature size,
in reality there are several, and they may not scale 1:1 to the new
process. So this adds to the re-layout, and, more important, can
change the circuit due to differing coupling between traces,
resitivity etc. So this may require a fair bit of work to get it back
on the line.

But the new Alphas ar not just a shrink; they are also going to a Cu
interconect, and to a slightly different CMOS. The Cu needs a total
re-layout to quite different rules to get the best from it, and this
would probably need a second tape-out before production. Also, the
silicon is different to the Intel and Samsung lines, so that has to be
factored in.

Also, you probably have minor changes you want to do. Some parameter
could be making life miserable, so a small shift could be a big win.
Real production could give you a different skew to the normal
disribution, so a tweek can get more devises into the useable range
for you system design.

Then, even if everything goes sweet in the fab, then you have to test
the little buggers. Can your, or your vendors testers cope? Have you
caused subtle timing changes that bite you, or cause race conditions?
It the yield enough to live with?

Then you get to fight putting them into the system!

Then you get to do it again...

BTW, anyone know the details of the package for the new 264? It seems
to be quite a change from what has been used so far.

