JF Mezei wrote:
> 
> Dirk Munk wrote:
> > We know that 1.6 GHz alpha's already were in existence more than a year ago. So
> > producing a reliable 1 GHz system should be possible by know. AFAIK the 1.01 GHz
> > ES45 should be out in a few weeks from now.
> 
> Intel seems to have very little difficulty in increasing the speed of its 8086
> at a rapid pace. is it correct to state that they get about 2 speed increases
> per year ? (in Mhz measures, not necessarily in performance).
> 
> Is there anything would would have prevented Compaq from producing 1ghz
> versions of the existing alpha chip, just like Intel produced 1+ ghz versions
> of its pentium III ?

I think you have inadvertently answered your own question. What version
of PIII was released over 1 GHz? The infamous 1.133 GHz part. Remember
what happened to it? That is why PC MPU speed grades can be bumped in
5 or 10% increments but server chips have fewer and bigger bumps - the
server chips have to be exhausitively tested, verified, and characterized.
First in silicon and then in systems and that can take the better part of
a year (much more if issues turn up - the whole process starts over again
after the fix).

> 
> I realise that the upcoming chip incldes design improvements, but from a pure
> mhz point of view, must the design be changed to allow greater speed, or just
> the manufacturing process ? (with perhaps minor tewaking of design).

It is a process port. In a full custom design that is uses aggressive
physical and circuit design like the EV6x core there is a hell of lot
of fine tuning to do. There was a paper at ISSCC in February on DECpaq's
port strategy to IBM's CU 0.18 um process including some of the circuit
changes that had to be made. At ISSCC 2000 there was a similar paper
for the port to 0.18 um aluminum.

Here's an example with numbers. The HP PA-8000 core is a complicated
beast like EV6 but doesn't quite use as much funky circuitry. When
HP ported it from their 0.5 um process to Intel's 0.25 um process
(PA-8500) it originally simulated maxxing out at 275 MHz (it ran
up to 180 MHz in 0.5 um). It took a heck of a lot of work to bring
it up to the 400 MHz targeted release frequency. It eventually
topped out at 440 MHz. More work and a Leff process tweak lifted it
to 552 MHz (PA-8600).

