According to a new Compaq Inspiration Technology radio commercial
running in the UK the greatest inventions that have been made possible
by the PC are the Internet, email and MP3. So any email or use of the
Internet you think you remember prior to the mid 80s was probably just
a result of too much acid in the 60s and 70s I guess.

More positively VMS has again increased in visibility in the latest
Issue of Inform. Front page of European Edition lists among the
contents "OpenVMS: Continuing the renaissance" and the US Edition
carries an expanded version of the same feature headlined on the front
page as: "OpenVMS: Back with a vengeance" 

Both the US and European editions carry a feature on the Eurex and
CBOT exchanges with VMS featuring heavily and there are  articles on
SAN support for VAX  in both editions The US edition also mentions
OpenVMS in use by Vodaphone for telecoms (strange this isn't in the
European edition as Vodaphone is a UK company although the article
does refer to Vodaphone in South Africa). Several other mentions of
VMS can be found throughout the issue and not just in the VMS specific
section.

One place where VMS prominently *does not* feature is Winkler's
article on "Where Compaq is Headed" (both editions - abbreviated
version in European edition). He talks about iPAQs, Proliant, Windows,
Alpha, Tru-64 but not OpenVMS (or Linux for that matter). This again
re-inforces my opinion that Winkler is no friend of VMS and he really
does not see a future for VMS with Compaq. To re-emphasize the article
is headed "Where Compaq is headed" in the US edition and there is no
VMS. It would have been so easy for him to add the words "and OpenVMS"
after Tru-64 Unix but he didn't.

Finally several Compaq ads appear in both publications (including
OpenVMS Times ad in European edition but not the US edition (well
online copy anyway) but the VMS search engine ad running in the UK
does not appear anywhere. Maybe next issue!

Overall VMS is at its highest visibility level for a long time in
Inform and this has to be a good thing. But just how do we get Winkler
on side? I've cc'd this to both Rich Marcello and Mike Winkler as
perhaps Mike just really doesn't realize just how sensitive the
customer base is given past (mainly DEC) treatment of VMS customers.

http://www.compaq.com/inform or
http://www.compaq.com/inform/in33final.pdf for complete magazine. Try
a search for VMS and you might be surprised
--
Alan


