We have a limited supply of parts to upgrade DEC Celebris FX-2 or
Venturis FX-2 systems to run with the AMD 400MHz K6-2 processor.  The
main logic boards in these models of DEC computers were manufactured
with an Intel-standard voltage regulator socket, a white 30-pin socket
with two small hinged plastic latches.  The voltage regulator socket
is located right next to the processor socket.  Upgrading with the AMD
K6-2 is pretty easy:

1. Remove the old processor and heat sink (careful removing the
clip!).
2. Insert the AMD K6-2 in the processor socket.
3. Clip a cooling fan onto the processor, and connect the cooling fan
power lead to the motherboard fan power connector.
4. Insert a voltage regulator module (VRM) in the voltage regulator
socket, and latch it in securely.
5. Set the system bus clock to 66MHz and the processor clock
multiplier to 2x(!).

The bad news is that if you are running the original Windows 95 "A"
release, you FIRST have to upgrade the operating system properly.  If
you are running the Windows 95 "B" or "C" OEM releases, you need to
install a Microsoft-supplied patch to correct a Windows design defect
regarding faster AMD processors.  If you are running Windows 98, ME,
or later, you don't need to worry about the operating system.

Here are the parts we have available with their prices, until we run
out:

AMD 400MHz K6-2 processor $40 (very limited)
Intel-standard voltage regulator module (VRM) supporting voltages from
2.1v to 3.5v in tenths of a volt $25
CPU Cooler heat sink/cooling fan $5

You can also buy all three for $67, or VRM and cooling fan for $29.
Quantity discounts for for very large quantities.  These prices are
already very competitive.

Add exact shipping.  One year warranty.  Minimum order $25.  In other
words, I won't sell you one or two heat sink/cooling fans, unless you
want to pick them up here and pay cash.  Not worth the time for me to
do the paperwork and packing to ship.

The voltage regulator module was manufactured by DEC for use in its
own computers.  We will send your voltage regulators preset for 2.2v
operation, and include complete documentation for all jumper settings.

When we run out of AMD processors, I have no objection if you buy the
processors elsewhere and get the other stuff from us.  Same if you
already have an AMD processor.

This is a tried and proven solution, but it works ONLY at 400MHz, due
to an oddity in the AMD processor pin signals for clock multiplier.
If you can get an AMD K6-2 CPU rated at a FASTER speed, it will also
work at 400MHz.  I don't know about the AMD K6-3 processor.  It sucks
a lot of wattage compared to the K6-2.

Other older models of DEC systems have the same voltage regulator
socket, but I have not tested them, so no guarantees.

