> According to an electrician I know, these things tend not to last too
> long when electric motors are powered through them.  Do the units in the
> Netherlands need to be replaced from time to time?

No, I don't think so. They can only be replaced by a electrician, because you have to
remove the main fuse(s) from the sealed main fusebox first. But in a normal household
there are not that many electric motors. I can only think of washing machines, dryers,
dishwashers, vacuum cleaners etc. It may well be that these automatic switches are not
used in industrial environments, because of the bigger power surges when a big electric
motor starts. Normal fuses in a house are medium fast (= max 50% overload), and for some
electric motors they use slow (=100% overload) fuses.





