Bob Koehler wrote:
> 
> In article <3ADBD77B.2F6E3CE6@home.nl>, Dirk Munk <munk@home.nl> writes:
> >
> > In The Netherlands it is required for the whole house to be protected by these
> > switches. In my own home for instance I have in my switchbox 7 groups protected by
> > 16A automatic switches. They will break the power by overload, or  when there is
> > more than 30mA difference between the hot wire and neutral.
> 
> According to an electrician I know, these things tend not to last too
> long when electric motors are powered through them.  Do the units in the
> Netherlands need to be replaced from time to time?
> 

No, not that I know. Perhaps if the circuit breaker trips several times
at high (over)load it will be damaged by sparking. This should not
happen frequently, hence the delay (or something like 50% margin) to
allow the current surge when a high power motor starts. 
I have never seen the design of those switches, but I suppose that the
measurement of the overload and the fault current to ground is done by
two separate systems, probably small induction coils that do *not* 
carry the full current of the circuit themselves.


