> John E. Malmberg <malmberg@encompasserve.organization> wrote:
>
>> For those of you who have not had formal training with using higher
>> than 20A house hold currents, and are toying with the idea of getting
>> their own 3-phase toys:
>
> In the case of my three phase toy, a VAX 6000, I've been told that it only
> takes about 600-700W to run diskless config I have.  At 208V, that's
> around 3-3.5A.  So even if the power consumption is say 5A, I should be
> able to handle that fairly neatly on a common 15A or 20A circuit.

The primary advantage of 3 phase is that for high currents, it is more
cost efficient than single phase.  As such, getting a low current feed may
not be cost effective.

It is not always an issue of how much current you can or do use, it is
how much current will you get fed when a short occurs.

>> The symptom of "infant mortality" for a new breaker can be that
>> it explodes with the force of a hand grenade.  Professional Electricians
>> really do use "10 foot poles" to touch these under load, and stand
>> to the side, out of the potential blast zone.
>
> Is that relatively common?  Even at 1 in a 1000 or 1 in 10,000 odds, I'm
> guessing this would be grounds for some extremely costly lawsuits directed
> towards the manufacturers of those breakers.

You have probably touched on one of the reasons that type of equipment
is so much more expensive than stuff rated for standard home use.

This can lead to home hackers taking shortcuts to same money, resulting
in very unsafe configurations.


I do not know the exact failure rates.  I do remember that in the IBEW
newsletters that I used to receive, it was one of the most emphasized
safety warnings.  As I recall, specific accidents were also reported.

The amount a accidents reported were high enough to give me a healthy
respect for distribution panels.

I would rather have life and limb than an insurance settlement for the
rare malfunction.

When you move from standard house circuits into things like three-phase,
the safety rules change, and the risks associated get higher.

I will admit that there are probably many people on this mailing list
/ news group that know how to safely handle higher currents and voltages.

Others may have just been lucky.

I just have seen enough hazzards created by people that should have known
better with just standard 110 volt circuits, and when those cheats
are attempted with higher currents, a disaster is just waiting to
happen.

If someone needs to ask questions about converting 3 phase, or connecting
it up inside a residential environment, it is a strong indication that they
do not have the required safety training for use of those circuits.

-John
