Well, I don't know about others in this thread, but those are all very
good reasons why I haven't even considered using high-current 3-phase
circuits with my VAX.  Especially after being told that you can't legally
get 3-phase power to residences in the US.  The hacks for the VAX (because
of it's low current requirements and the design of it's power systems)
involve using one relatively common single-phase low current circuit at
240V.  Still, nothing to toy with, but it's within the "danger realm" of
hooking up an electric dryer in your basement.

The only 3-phase option I've considered using is a single-phase to 3-phase
motor generator, available commercially, and one which is small enough to
be unable to generate much current beyond that which is needed by the VAX.  
And obviously less current than what the supplying circuit is capable of
providing.

The first option involves mucking around with the guts of the VAX a bit
more than I'd like, and the second is relatively inefficient in terms of
energy consumption.

