On Fri, 13 Apr 2001, Brian Chase wrote:

> I'm looking to run a VAX 6000 using standard residental electrical service
> (United States flavor).  I know you can modify the power supplies on the
> VAX 6000 to directly accept single phase power, but I'm a little reluctant
> to perform the hack--even though I know people have had good success with
> it.
> 
> I know there exist single phase to 3-pase power converters... basically
> it's a 3-phase generator that's powered by single phase power.  I've found
> two sites which offer such converters (http://www.phaseconverter.com and
> http://www.majorpower.com/phase ).  The sites only mention their use to
> power things like 3-phase machine shop equipment and other
> industrial/manufacturing type gear.
> 
> Is it safe to use this type of power converter on a somewhat more delicate
> piece of equipment like a VAX 6000?  Has anyone had experience with using
> this type of power converter on their computer systems?
> 
> Thanks.
> 
> -brian.

I don't know if it would be safe or not, but I don't think it would be
very efficient.  I think it would be cheaper in the long run (maybe
the short run) to get 3-phase power installed.  I think most modern
electrical service is already 3-phase, so you would just need an
electrician to run a suitable circuit from your fuse-box/breaker-panel.

If you don't have 3-phase power, it is certainly 3-phase at the street
and the electric company could hook it up to your house, but you would
probably need to replace your service panel, or at least have it re-wired
to balance the loads.

I just checked, and it looks like there are 3 wires coming into my house
from the pole, which I think means 3-phase.  (I think the neutral is
carried by the ground and not by a separate wire.)  However, I live
in a duplex with two electric meters, so I suppose they could be two
hots plus a neutral (each apartment on a separate phase), or two legs
of the 3-phase plus a neutral, just to provide us with 220v for the
clothes driers.

There are a lot of old hardware types who hang out on alt.sys.pdp11
and/or vmsnet.pdp11, so it might be a good idea to ask there.

