I have no mouth and I must scream...

The RRD40-DA is definitely a SCSI device, SCSI-2 to be precise. Some did
have a very strange cable connector, I've heard it described as a SCSI-1
connector.  It looks like a DB25 on steroids with about 3 times the number
of pins.  I've also seen it with a regular SCSI-2 cable connector.  It had
a very unusual caddy, shaped like a pair of  hooks around the disk to
insert into the drive.  The caddy broke very easily, and are very hard to
find these days.  It was only a 1 or 2x drive, so speedy it is not.
If someone is trying to sell you one and you are not a DEC museum, then
pass.  If you've recently discovered one on the back shelf in the dark
closet, it's a nifty toy to play with.  Do you want to hook it up to your
gigahertz network, No.
The RRD42 and the RRD43 were much better, and can be found for very little
money.

Good luck,

Tom C.

