I Don't know about the US situation, but in Europe a full 3-phase connection has
5 wires.
There are 3 phases, 1 neutral and 1 ground wire. The voltage between the phases
is 400 volts, and between a phase and neutral is 230 volts.
Some equipment only uses 4 wires (3 phases and ground), so that is equipment
running on 3 phase 400 volts.
Other equipment (like VAX 6000 systems over here) use 5 wires and run on 3 phase
230 volts.
In Holland we call a 3 phase 380 volts connection a triangle connection , and a
3 phase 230 volts connection a star connection (the centre of the star being the
neutral, and the points of the star / triangle are the phases).
If you draw it out on paper you will find the root-3 (=1.73) relationship
between 230 and 400 volts.



