In the US, homes are wired with 120/240 volt single phase service.  Large
apartment/condo complexes, high rise apartment/condo buildings and most
commercial locations have 120/208 3 phase service.  (Individual dwelling units
will still have only single phase service.)  Most streets in residential
locations have only single phase power available.  If that is the case at your
house, the electric company will need to install wiring for the other two phases
on your street.  They will then need to install additional transformers for your
house.  I think most electric companies will charge you for that.  You will need
to have an electrician install a 3 phase electrical panel in your house.  I
think you are looking at a cost of several thousand dollars.  If you really want
to find out the cost you should be able to get a free estimate from an
electrician.  You will need to know the voltage and current rating  of each
"device".   (CPU cabinet, expansion cabinet, etc.).  I thing the large cost of a
3 phase service is why there is so much interest in converting the large systems
from three phase to single phase power. John Santos wrote:

>  Is it safe to use this type of power converter on a somewhat more delicate
> piece of equipment like a VAX 6000?
It will most likely need to provide a clean "true sine wave" output and able to
handle a non-linear load.

