  Be careful, here...

  The rackmount you refer to was the Voice Product Group's (DECtalk 
  Engineering's) DF108-RM variant of the original DF-series DF100-RM 
  rack, with the extra power needed for the DTC03 modules.  Later 
  DF-series modems also required more power, which meant that there 
  was a move to re-use the DECtalk DF108-RM rack for the DF-series 
  modems.  (The DTC03 was a module with the same form factor as the 
  DF-series rack-mount modems.)  

  We also had a few DTC03-AM modules mounted inside the standard DF-series 
  desktop modem enclosure (the DF100-DT box) during the original DECtalk
  DTC03 engineering effort.  I would NOT recommend this, as the DF100-DT 
  lacked the necessary cooling for DTC03, was likely to toast the DTC03 
  module, and was accordingly never sold nor supported.

  All that aid, the DECtalk-I DTC01 that the original poster is refering to 
  is rather different from the DECtalk-III DTC03 discussed above.  The 
  DECtalk-I DTC01 was mounted inside a VT240-series terminal system unit, 
  and was not available in a rack-mount configuration.

  As for the feature differences, the salient difference between the DTC01
  and the DTC03 involved the physical mountings mentioned earlier, and the 
  call progress detection capabilities.  The DTC01 had only blind-dial and 
  was not particularly suited to use when placing outgoing telephone calls, 
  while the DTC03 had at least basic call progress detection capabilities 
  and could rather more easily place and manage outgoing telephone calls.


