On Fri, 13 Apr 2001, Dirk Munk wrote:
> I Don't know about the US situation, but in Europe a full 3-phase
> connection has 5 wires.

John Santos <JOHN@egh.com> writes:
> That's definitely true of 3-phase internal wiring here in the US.

Yes.  Most US internal 3-phase wiring is three-phase-wye, with three phases,
a neutral, and a ground.  However, the actual three-phase outlets may
be four-wire, for devices that don't need neutral.  Older outlets may omit
ground.

> What I was describing is the connection from the power pole to the
> house.  I'm not certain, but I think 1) the electric distribution grid
> uses the earth (the planet, that is) as the neutral,

I've seen that claimed many times, but AFAIK in the majority of cases
that's not what's really being done.  The distribution system uses
three-phase-delta, which has no neutral.  At some point closer to where
the power is consumed, there is a three-phase transformer with the
primary wired in a delta configuration, and the secondary in a wye
configuration.  This "creates" a neutral, which is then tied to ground
(at only a single point; elsewhere neutral may have a different voltage).

For instance, in one building I'm familiar with, the utility provides
three-phase-delta at 480V phase-to-phase (which would be 277V
phase-to-neutral if there was a neutral).  Inside the building there is
a three-phase transformer that steps it down to 208V phase-to-phase,
which is 120V phase-to-neutral.

> and 2) the "ground" wire is used inside buildings for safety, is tied
> to the ground at exactly one point (within a short distance of where
> the power enters the building), normally carries no current, and
> doesn't exist outside a building.

True.  When the ground conductor is carrying current, there is a fault
condition somewhere.  Since there is normally no current, the grounds on
all of your outlets are very close to "true" ground (0V) potential.

The reason that neutral may have a non-zero voltage is that it may
carry current, and there will be voltage losses since the conductor
has some resistance.  If you have three-phase with neutral, and the load
is perfectly balanced, the neutral current will be zero.  However, often
the load is not perfectly balanced, especially when the phases are used
for independent single-phase loads.

> But don't listen to me - consult an electrician.

Good advice.


