You are absolutely right, and that is why neutral is not connected to ground
at all in the house itself. As far as I know neutral is only connected to the
ground at the power source, so at some big transformer of the power company.
And I have been told this is only to prevent the whole system from "floating"
in respect to the ground potential.

For security reasons the switch boxes in the house are also equiped with
"earth leakage switches" (don't know what the English verb for such a switch
is). It measures the difference between the incoming current over the phase,
and the outgoing current over neutral. As soon as a difference of more than 30
mA is detected, (thus a current of more than 30 mA is leaking to ground
somewhere), the group or the whole power is switched off.



