"David J. Dachtera" <djesys.nospam@fsi.net> writes:

>We call that a GFCI - Ground Fault Current Interruptor

>...IMO, something of a misnomer in some cases because the GFCI breakers
>I installed this past season have no connection to the earth ground,
>only the "hot" and "neutral" wires. Maybe "neutral fault current
>interruptor" would be more appropriate.

No, ground fault circuit interrupter is correct since its purpose in life
is to detect ground faults.  You are correct in that they don't use
a ground connector since it's nearly impossible to measure the current
going to ground, it would only work if the fault went to the ground wire
itself.  But these devices have to be useful to protect against someone
using a hair dryer in a bathtub, or a circular saw outside while standing
in a puddle.  They work by measuring the difference between the hot and
neutral leads, any difference means a fault.

I suppose that technically they detect any kind of fault such as to
another neutral or hot lead so not all faults caught would be a "ground"
fault.  Just 99.9% of them.

-Mike


