It is too bad that Sun has given up on the technical computing market
because unfortunately for some reason many companies still haven't
got the message and continue to buy Sun products for engineers. The
harnessing a hundred chickens approach to "high end computing" doesn't
cut it and the engineering foot soldiers suffer greatly as a result.
You are right about software. ECAD vendors *LUV* Sun. God knows
they wouldn't sell nearly as many CPU licenses to customer sites
if they ported to Alpha or P4/Linux.  ;^)

I have a 3 year old entry level PA-RISC workstation under my desk
that the local Sun IS mafiosa has been trying to replace for several
years. Too bad nothing short of an Sun_unobtainium box (US-III) would
be an improvement for what I do. Certainly not the any of the US-II
"upgrades" I have successfully fought off. I suspect even if we got
US-III boxes in for the full custom chip designers that my T-bird
would probably run my circuit sims faster. Especially with the US-III
bug "fixed" by turning instruction prefetch off.

BTW, one of the RWT articles I am working on compares Sun/SPARC today
with DEC/VAX at its high water mark. There are so many striking
similarities it is remarkable - under powered processor propelled
by strong networking, solid OS, SMP systems, clustering, and good
software availability (it isn't perfect, VAXen were highly reliable).
But I haven't seen a VAX in more than a decade. When the flock wake
up to alternatives the tide turns pretty fast!


