Brian Chase wrote:
> 
> In article <GBFwvA.Gxs@world.std.com>, Brian Chase <bdc@world.std.com> wrote:
> Okay... well, I've gotten some feedback so far, but it's all still sort of
> fuzzy.  Here's some more information...  I've got a VAX 6000-420,
> apparently an older style one.  It uses MS62-xx type memory.  I have some
> VAX 6000-600 series CPUs on the way, as well as some newer RAM that works
> with those CPUs.
> 
> I've been told, that I may need to modify the backplane (Is this a simple
> replacement of the cardcage, do I need different power supplies?).  I've
> also been told that there existed an XMI module which could plugged into
> the old XMI backplane to allow the the VAX 6000-600 CPUs to work in it.
> More details on either or both of these options would be very much
> appreciated.
> 
> Really, I just need a list of all the parts I need to take a 420 to a 620.
> I have the CPUs, I have the RAM.  What else?

The VAX 6000-500 and VAX 6000-600 shipped with 
what (IIRC) was referred to as the XMI-2 platform
whereas the previous VAX 6000 shipped with the
XMI-1 platform.

XMI-2 added a few bits and bobs over XMI-1.
From a hardware point of view you got an
extra supply voltage (+3.3V, perhaps a
few others too); from a software
point of view you could support machines
with > 512MB of physical memory.

VAX 6000-600 supported up to 3.5GB of
memory (again, IIRC); the VAX 6000-500
could have supported more than 512MB
but I don't think that ever happened.

Older systems' platforms could be converted
in the field from XMI-1 to XMI-2. I have
the manual that describes this somewhere
but it essentially means removing the XMI-1
card cage and one PSU and swapping in a
new backplane and PSU. This is not the path
you wish to take (I assume).

The simpler upgrade is to use a T2019.
This is an XMI module that you
install in slot 2 (it make slot 1 unusable
too because of its size). This module
supplies +3.3V on the relevant XMI
lines. You need a connector at the back
too to feed the new power down the correct
lines.

There are some limitations with the T2019
method. You are limited to four of the
newer processors rather than eight and
you cannot have a battery backup.

There are some options which will specifically
bot function in an environment where +3.3V
is supplied (because the +3.3V appears on lines
that were previously ground). The KA62A, KA62B
and KA64A processors, MS62A memory and the 
DWMBA XMI-BI adapter.

If you have a true XMI-2 platform
(or a fully upgraded XMI-1 platform)
I think that the system protects you
from damaging such components if they
are present (by not powering up).

If you use the T2019, I don't know
whether you get that protection.

So I think the short answer is: T2019
and that widget you plug in at the back
(sorry, no part number ... I can hit the
IPBs if you think you might have it
but are not sure).

Antonio

